# RK-ZYNQ7100 release @VERSION@

Board images and the corresponding source snapshot. Rebuilding also needs
the tools and sibling checkout listed below. Built from commit `@COMMIT@`.

The board is an AMD Zynq-7000 `xc7z100ffg900-2` with 1 GB of PS DDR3, 1 GB of
PL DDR3, HDMI in and out, a MIPI camera connector, two SFP+ cages, two
Gigabit Ethernet ports, a 40-pin header, eMMC, QSPI and micro-SD.

## What is here

| | |
|---|---|
| `bitstreams/rkz7100-board.bit`, `.bin` | The whole board in one design: the PL DDR3, the register file, HDMI in and out, the camera, the SFP transceivers, PL Ethernet, the 40-pin header, the fan and three memory test engines |
| `bitstreams/rkz7100-ddr.bit`, `.bin` | The PL DDR3 and its test engines alone -- the minimal design for bringing a MIG up (present when the release was built with `--ddr`) |
| `bitstreams/board-timing.txt`, `board-utilization.txt` | Vivado's timing and utilization reports for the board bitstream |
| `linux/` | U-Boot, the kernel, three device trees and a root filesystem (`rootfs.ext4.gz`), plus `sdcard.img.gz` |
| `source/` | The repository this was built from, exactly as committed |
| `SHA256SUMS` | A checksum for every file above |

`.bit` is for JTAG and Vivado; `.bin` is the byte order Linux's
`fpga_manager` takes. `source/pl/bit2bin.py` converts one to the other.

## Running it without building anything

**From an SD card.** Write `linux/sdcard.img.gz` to a card -- `zcat
linux/sdcard.img.gz | sudo dd of=/dev/sdX bs=4M conv=fsync`, or balenaEtcher,
which takes the `.gz` as it is -- set SW1 to SD (both switches off) and power
up. The console is the second of the two serial ports the JTAG USB-C port (USB1)
adds, 115200 8N1. It
boots to a login prompt; the user is `root` with no password. ⚠ The card image
is checked by the packager but has not yet been booted on the board itself;
the flash and network boots below have.

The card carries the board bitstream, `rkz7100.bin` on its FAT partition, and
U-Boot loads it before the kernel starts, so the PL is configured before Linux
probes the devices that live on it. To run a different design, replace that
file; the `.bin` files in `bitstreams/` are already in the form it takes.

**From the board's own flash.** See "Installing to eMMC and QSPI" in
`source/linux/README.md`. The board then boots with nothing attached. There,
the bitstream U-Boot loads is `/boot/rkz7100.bin` on the eMMC; put one there
from `bitstreams/`.

⚠ **Do not read a PL address before a bitstream is loaded.** The access is
answered by nothing and the CPU stops there -- no message, no recovery but a
reset.

## Checking the hardware

`source/pl/tools/` and `source/pl/ddr/test-ddr.sh` hold a script for each part
of the board. They print what they measured, not just a verdict:

```
sh bisttest.sh          # the memory test engines, and their interrupts
sh ../ddr/test-ddr.sh -b 0x80100000   # the PL DDR3 (memtest too, if built beside it)
sh hdmitest.sh          # HDMI out: the transmitter's PLL and its setup
sh hdmirxtest.sh -c     # HDMI in: capture frames and check the pattern
sh camtest.sh           # the camera, in every sensor mode
sh gtxtest.sh -r all    # the SFP+ transceivers at all three line rates
sh eth2test.sh          # the PL Ethernet port at 1000, 100 and 10 Mb/s
sh j8test.sh            # all 34 pins of the 40-pin header
```

The HDMI tests need a cable from HDMI1 to HDMI2, the SFP test a cable between
the two cages, and the camera test a module in J9.

## Rebuilding

Clone `retrosim-build` beside `source/` before rebuilding or running the
simulation gate, and install Python 3.10 or later. Follow the shared tool
setup linked from `source/README.md`; the sibling is not bundled here.

**The PL** needs AMD Vivado 2026.1, because the IP versions are fixed:

```
vivado -mode batch -source source/pl/board/build.tcl -tclargs <build dir>
```

For the GUI, add `project` and open the `.xpr` it names:

```
vivado -mode batch -source source/pl/board/build.tcl -tclargs <build dir> project
```

Both read the same file lists, so the project and the batch build cannot
disagree.

**The simulation gate** additionally needs Icarus Verilog; Verilator enables
optional lint, and Yosys enables the block-RAM check. It is the thing to run before a Vivado build:

```
sh source/pl/sim/run.sh
```

**Linux** is built by Buildroot; see `source/linux/README.md`.

## Licensing

Original hardware designs, scripts and documents use MIT OR Apache-2.0, at
your option. `source/README.md` lists exceptions, including GPL device trees,
kernel patches and generated PS initialization files, plus vendor-derived
board data. Review that license section and each file's notice before
redistributing. Generated `ps7_init.tcl` is excluded from this source snapshot
and the release artifacts. The PL build generates it locally for JTAG boot;
`source/doc/linux-guide.md` describes standalone generation as well.
`source/LICENSE-MIT` and `source/LICENSE-APACHE` contain the license texts.

The binaries in `linux/` are a different matter. The kernel, U-Boot and
BusyBox are GPL-licensed, and their sources are published beside this
release as `rkz7100-@VERSION@-gpl-sources.tar.gz` -- every upstream tarball
Buildroot used, with its manifest. `linux/gpl-manifest.csv` lists what is in
it: package, version, license and where it came from. If that archive is not
beside this one, the binaries should not be redistributed.

The bitstreams contain AMD/Xilinx IP, which its license allows to be used on
Xilinx devices.

## Where the detail is

| | |
|---|---|
| `source/README.md` | what the board is and what has been measured on it |
| `source/doc/RK_ZYNQ7100_User_Guide.md` | the board itself: power, clocks, banks, every peripheral with pin tables, and the known issues |
| `source/pl/README.md` | the PL designs: address map, registers, and every block with its measurements |
| `source/linux/README.md` | the Linux port, booting, and installing to flash |
| `source/pl/sim/README.md` | the benches, and what each is built to catch |
